= S4. Repository development by topic and package (main-text Fig. 4)

== S4.1 Pull-request topics over time (Fig. 4a)

Pull requests (PRs) in all states were collected from `materialsproject/pymatgen` through the paginated GitHub REST API on 8 September 2026. The figure includes all 2,494 non-bot PRs merged before 1 January 2026, including 167 merged during the complete 2025 calendar year. Accounts identified as bots by GitHub or whose usernames end in `[bot]` (case-insensitively) were excluded. Each PR number is retained once, including repeated titles, and years refer to merger dates.

Each PR title was classified directly using ordered, case-insensitive regular-expression rules. The first matching theme was assigned in this order: documentation; testing and code quality; performance; bug fixes and refactoring; I/O and parsing; structural and analysis functionality; and Other / unclassified for unmatched titles. Every PR is assigned exactly once, with no title deduplication or counts inferred from LLM-generated summaries. These are approximate title-based classifications rather than manually validated descriptions of PR contents. The archived data retain each PR's number, title, author, dates, and assigned theme for inspection.

== S4.2 Package-level commit activity (Fig. 4b)

Non-merge commits were collected from the Git history of `materialsproject/pymatgen`, beginning at the initial commit `fa7f41d8bd769a04cca1f78242ebf072664c871d` on 23 October 2011 and ending on 31 December 2025. The figure excludes commits before 1 January 2012 and therefore shows activity from 2012 through 2025. Commits were counted for 13 selected paths: `command_line`, `symmetry`, `alchemy`, `core/structure.py`, `core/periodic_table.py`, `io/vasp`, `transformations`, `analysis`, `optimization`, `electronic_structure`, `phonon`, `vis`, and `entries`. Both `pymatgen/` and `src/pymatgen/` paths were matched over the full period (src layout adopted on 26 June 2024).

Commits touching each path were counted by month and, from January 2012 onward, aggregated into calendar half-years (January--June and July--December). A commit touching more than one selected path was counted once for each corresponding path.

= S5. Pull requests by contributor tenure (main-text Fig. 5)

The tenure figure uses the same 2,494 non-bot merged PRs and merger-year grouping as Fig. 4a. PRs in all states were retrieved so that each author's first submission date includes unmerged PRs. Tenure is the elapsed time from that first submission to the merger date of each included PR, rather than its creation date.

PRs were assigned to five disjoint tenure groups: less than 7 days; 7 days to less than 1 year; 1 to less than 3 years; 3 to less than 6 years; and at least 6 years. One year is defined as 365.25 days. All merges through 31 December 2025 are included. Only PR activity is represented; reviews, comments, and other contributions are not counted.

= S6. Geographic distribution of pull-request contributions (main-text Fig. 6)

The GitHub API was used to count merged PRs by author and retrieve each author's public, self-reported profile location. Accounts whose GitHub username ended in `[bot]`, matched case-insensitively, were excluded. Free-text locations were geocoded to countries with OpenStreetMap Nominatim. For unresolved contributors listed on the public pymatgen team page, the listed affiliations were mapped to countries using OpenAI's GPT-4o model. Additional mappings were manually curated. Contributors without a country mapping were omitted from the country totals. The PR counts and profile locations were retrieved around 16 February 2026.

Of 276 contributors, 124 (44.9%) provided no location information in their GitHub profile. All 152 contributors who provided location information were assigned a country, including through manually curated geocoding results. Institutional affiliations listed on the public pymatgen team page, together with manually curated mappings, supplied countries for 57 of the 124 contributors without a profile location. The final country mapping covered 209 contributors (75.7%) and 2,362 of 2,498 merged PRs (94.6%). The remaining 67 contributors (24.3%), accounting for 136 merged PRs (5.4%), had no country mapping.

PR counts were summed by mapped country. The resulting values are numbers of merged PRs, not numbers of unique contributors. A contributor's mapped country was based on the public profile location or listed affiliation used during data collection.

= S7. Source-code size and test coverage (main-text Fig. 7)

Both treemap areas and coverage percentages were derived from the coverage.py 7.6.12 #link("https://github.com/user-attachments/files/21545087/2025-07-31-pymatgen-coverage.json")[JSON snapshot] generated on 31 July 2025 with branch coverage disabled. Areas count executable statements in reported Python files under `src/pymatgen/`; files with no executable statements are omitted, while files with zero covered statements are retained. Colors represent summed covered statements divided by summed executable statements, including for parent packages. The report records 60,242 covered statements out of 76,389 executable statements (78.9%). Coverage is 90.2% for `core`, 85.8% for `entries`, 83.6% for `io`, and 13.9% for `vis`. Cells are grouped to at most three path components; groups below 1,500 statements are combined into other cells, and subpackages below 5,000 statements are shown as single cells. No separately installed pymatgen source tree is used for sizing; the figure describes this dated test-suite snapshot.

= S8. Contributor and commit activity (main-text Fig. 8)

Non-merge commit metadata were collected from the Git history of `materialsproject/pymatgen` from 1 January 2012 through 31 December 2025. Merge commits were excluded. Authors whose names ended in `[bot]`, matched case-insensitively, were also excluded.

Author records with an identical name or lower-cased email address were assigned a common contributor identifier. Non-merge commit counts were aggregated by calendar year according to the Git author date. A contributor with at least one non-merge commit in a given year was counted once for that year. The solid line reports the number of active contributors, and the dotted line reports the total number of non-merge commits in the same year. Only activity recorded in the Git commit history was included.
